export const DOCS: Record<string, { title: string; html: string }> = {"getting-started":{"title":"Getting started","html":"<h1>Getting started</h1><p>Amethyst is an ADE (Agentic Development Environment) for Windows 10 and 11. You run your own coding agents in parallel on your own accounts, keep every project in one window, and switch between tasks without stopping running agents.</p><h2>1. Install Amethyst</h2><p>1. Download <code>Amethyst-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code> from the latest GitHub Release and run it.</p><p>2. The installer is not code-signed yet, so Windows SmartScreen may show a warning. Click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</p><p>3. The installer also places <code>amethyst.cmd</code> in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Amethyst\\bin</code> on your <code>PATH</code> so you can use the <code>amethyst</code> command from any new terminal.</p><p>You can use the editor, terminals, boards, and project explorer without signing in to any agent.</p><h2>2. Sign in to at least one coding agent</h2><p>Amethyst runs each agent through its own CLI on your own account. Install and sign in to whichever agents you already use:</p><ul><li><strong>Claude:</strong> install Claude Code and sign in (<code>claude auth login</code>), or enter an Anthropic API key, Console account, Bedrock, Vertex, or Foundry setup in Amethyst.</li><li><strong>Codex:</strong> install the Codex CLI and run <code>codex login</code>, or use an API key.</li><li><strong>Antigravity:</strong> install the Antigravity CLI (<code>agy</code>) and sign in with your Google account or Gemini API key.</li><li><strong>Cursor:</strong> install the Cursor CLI (<code>agent</code>) and sign in, or use an API key.</li><li><strong>GitHub Copilot:</strong> install the Copilot CLI (<code>copilot</code>) and sign in, or use a GitHub token.</li></ul><p>See <strong>Agent setup and accounts</strong> for managing multiple Claude accounts, usage limits, and per-agent skills and instructions.</p><h2>3. Set up your first workspace and project</h2><ul><li>A <strong>project</strong> is any folder on disk (usually a git repository). Each project keeps its own chats, tabs, terminals, and state in a <code>.amethyst/</code> folder.</li><li>A <strong>workspace</strong> is a collection of projects shown together in the project dock at the bottom of the window.</li></ul><p>Add your project folder to a workspace, then click projects in the dock (or press <code>Alt+Shift+Left</code> / <code>Alt+Shift+Right</code>) to switch in one click. Chats and terminals run in Amethyst's local server, so agents in other projects keep working while you switch.</p><h2>4. Start your first chats</h2><ul><li>Press <code>Ctrl+R</code> (or click <strong>+ agent</strong> in the left rail) to open a new chat, or use <code>Ctrl+1</code> through <code>Ctrl+5</code> to start a specific agent directly (<code>1</code> Claude, <code>2</code> Codex, <code>3</code> Antigravity, <code>4</code> Cursor, <code>5</code> Copilot).</li><li>In the chat box, choose your model, reasoning effort, and permission mode (or cycle them with <code>Alt+C</code>, <code>Alt+Z</code>, and <code>Alt+M</code>).</li><li>Use <code>@</code> to reference files, folders, or other chats by their short code (like <code>Q20</code>), and paste or drop up to 6 images per message.</li><li>If a chat is busy, you can still send follow-up messages — they wait in its queue, and you can take them back or send one immediately.</li></ul><h2>5. Run several tasks in parallel</h2><ul><li><strong>Agent decks (<code>Alt+A</code>):</strong> arrange live chats in sets. Chats waiting for your answer are listed first; press <code>Alt+N</code> to jump straight to the next chat that needs you.</li><li><strong>The bench (<code>Alt+B</code>):</strong> move chats that can wait out of view while they keep running, and bring them back when you are ready.</li><li><strong>Worktrees:</strong> toggle the worktree button in a new chat's box before sending its first message so it works on its own branch in an isolated folder. See <strong>Worktrees and context handoff</strong>.</li><li><strong>Review and Rewind:</strong> inspect every file a chat changed in the Edits strip or Review window, and rewind both the chat and files if an approach does not pan out. See <strong>Reviewing edits and rewind</strong>.</li></ul><h2>6. Make the window yours</h2><ul><li><strong>Hide anything you do not use:</strong> right-click almost any button, panel, or bar to hide it for good (or browse all toggles in <strong>Settings -&gt; Show and hide</strong>).</li><li><strong>Command palette:</strong> press <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> to find any command and its shortcut, or <code>Ctrl+P</code> to open any file.</li><li><strong>Rebind keys and themes:</strong> customize themes, accent colors, syntax colors, and shortcuts in <strong>Settings</strong>.</li></ul>"},"modes-and-shortcuts":{"title":"Modes and shortcuts","html":"<h1>Modes, browser tab, quick tools, and shortcuts</h1><p>Amethyst is built around switching cleanly between views of your work without losing state or stopping running agents.</p><h2>Workspace modes</h2><p>Switch between modes from the left rail or with shortcuts:</p><ul><li><strong>Editor (<code>Alt+E</code>):</strong> CodeMirror 6 editor with splits (<code>Ctrl+\\</code>), tab groups, diff tabs, Markdown preview, image/PDF viewing, and chats beside your code. Press <code>Alt+E</code> again to cycle saved editor layouts (<code>Alt+Shift+E</code> creates a new layout).</li><li><strong>Agents (<code>Alt+A</code>):</strong> a live grid of chats organized into sets, with a spotlight column and a bench (<code>Alt+B</code>) for chats that keep running out of view. Press <code>Alt+A</code> again to cycle sets (<code>Alt+Shift+A</code> creates a new set).</li><li><strong>Terminals (<code>Alt+T</code>):</strong> real PowerShell and Command Prompt terminals (<code>xterm.js</code> + <code>node-pty</code>) arranged in sets, plus one-click launchers for agent CLIs (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>agy</code>, <code>cursor</code>, <code>copilot</code>, <code>gemini</code>). Terminals keep running when you switch projects or close the window to the tray.</li><li><strong>Research (<code>Alt+R</code>):</strong> follow one chat alongside its live subagents and files in up to 4 tab groups side by side.</li><li><strong>Orchestration (<code>Alt+Q</code>):</strong> manage all active chats in the project in one calm overview, including benched chats and chats waiting for your input.</li><li><strong>The Mind:</strong> a visual canvas of your project's folders and files where recently touched files stand out.</li></ul><h2>Built-in browser tab</h2><p>Amethyst includes an embedded browser tab for checking local dev servers or docs right beside your agents:</p><ul><li><strong>Element picker:</strong> click the pick tool and click any element on the page to send its URL, CSS selector, visible text, and HTML snippet directly into a chat.</li><li><strong>Draw and send:</strong> annotate directly on a web page and send the marked-up screenshot straight to a chat.</li><li><strong>Hard refresh:</strong> press <code>Ctrl+Shift+R</code> to reload the browser tab.</li></ul><h2>Quick tools (from anywhere)</h2><p>Each quick tool opens in its own small window and works from any mode:</p><ul><li><strong>Quick Agent (<code>Ctrl+Shift+Space</code>):</strong> pop open a fast chat for the current project.</li><li><strong>Quick Terminal (`<code> Ctrl+</code> `<code>) / Quick PowerShell (</code>Ctrl+~`):</strong> toggle a quick shell in the current project.</li><li><strong>Scratch Pad (<code>Alt+S</code>):</strong> jot notes or prompts down immediately, then turn a note into a real file when ready.</li><li><strong>Sketch Board (<code>Alt+D</code>):</strong> open a freehand drawing board tab or window.</li><li><strong>Inbox (<code>Alt+I</code>):</strong> open your inbox folder to review captured files.</li><li><strong>Don't Forget (<code>Ctrl+J</code>):</strong> create reminders with timers, repeats, and snooze.</li><li><strong>Clips:</strong> save and reuse snippets.</li><li><strong>Freeze Log:</strong> automatically records slow frames, clicks, and stalls. Save a 40-second snapshot (with CPU profiles and system load) to attach to a bug report or hand directly to an agent to diagnose performance issues.</li></ul><h2>Default keyboard shortcuts</h2><p>Every shortcut can be rebound in <strong>Settings</strong>. Press <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> anytime to search all commands.</p><h3>Navigation and modes</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code></td><td>Command palette</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+P</code></td><td>Quick open file</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+E</code> / <code>Alt+Shift+E</code></td><td>Editor mode (cycle layouts) / new editor layout</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+A</code> / <code>Alt+Shift+A</code></td><td>Agents mode (cycle sets) / new agents set</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+T</code></td><td>Terminals mode</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+R</code></td><td>Research mode</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+Q</code></td><td>Orchestration mode</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+X</code></td><td>Close or clear current set/layout</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+Shift+Left</code> / <code>Alt+Shift+Right</code></td><td>Previous / next project</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Chats and decks</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Alt+N</code></td><td>Jump to the next chat that needs you</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+J</code> / <code>Alt+K</code></td><td>Next / previous cell in a deck</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+Q</code></td><td>Add an empty cell to the current set</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+B</code></td><td>Bench the selected chat or terminal</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+H</code></td><td>Show / hide the agents or terminals list rail</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+R</code></td><td>New chat picker at pointer</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+1</code> .. <code>Ctrl+5</code></td><td>New chat: <code>1</code> Claude, <code>2</code> Codex, <code>3</code> Antigravity, <code>4</code> Cursor, <code>5</code> Copilot</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+M</code> / <code>Alt+Shift+M</code></td><td>Cycle permission mode in a chat's box</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+C</code> / <code>Alt+Z</code></td><td>Next / previous model in a chat's box</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+N</code></td><td>Show / hide a chat's note</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+F</code></td><td>Find in chat box or whole chat (press again to toggle)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+T</code></td><td>Clear chat (asks first)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Tabs and editor</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Ctrl+\\</code></td><td>Split editor right</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+W</code></td><td>Close tab</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+Shift+T</code></td><td>Reopen closed tab</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+1</code> .. <code>Alt+9</code></td><td>Switch to tab 1..9 in the active group</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alt+O</code></td><td>Switch between C/C++ header and source (<code>Foo.h</code> and <code>Foo.cpp</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},"agent-setup-and-accounts":{"title":"Agent setup and accounts","html":"<h1>Agent setup and accounts</h1><p>Amethyst reads each agent's native instructions, skills, rules, and memory files, lets you manage them from one place, and supports multiple Claude accounts with live usage tracking.</p><h2>Where each agent keeps its setup files</h2><p>Amethyst's Agent Setup view inspects the standard global and project paths for each supported agent:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Global instructions</th><th>Project instructions</th><th>Global skills</th><th>Project skills</th><th>Memory / rules notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Claude</strong></td><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> (in active account dir)</td><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> (at the project root or inside <code>.claude/</code>)</td><td><code>skills/</code> (in active account dir)</td><td><code>.claude/skills/</code></td><td>Also has <code>rules/</code> and per-project auto-memory files (<code>MEMORY.md</code>, etc.).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Codex</strong></td><td><code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code></td><td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td><code>~/.codex/skills/</code></td><td><code>.agents/skills/</code></td><td>Keeps memory internally rather than in files (<code>CODEX_HOME</code> overrides <code>~/.codex</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antigravity</strong></td><td><code>~/.gemini/GEMINI.md</code></td><td><code>GEMINI.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td><code>~/.gemini/config/skills/</code></td><td><code>.agents/skills/</code></td><td>Keeps no memory files; its <code>/learn</code> command writes skills and rules instead.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cursor</strong></td><td>(project only)</td><td><code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code></td><td><code>~/.cursor/skills/</code></td><td><code>.cursor/skills/</code></td><td>Project rules live in <code>.cursor/rules/</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GitHub Copilot</strong></td><td><code>~/.copilot/copilot-instructions.md</code></td><td><code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code></td><td><code>~/.copilot/skills/</code></td><td><code>.github/skills/</code></td><td>Keeps memory internally; also reads <code>.agents/skills</code>, <code>.claude/skills</code>, and <code>.github/instructions</code> (<code>COPILOT_HOME</code> overrides <code>~/.copilot</code>).</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each skill is a subfolder containing a <code>SKILL.md</code> file with YAML frontmatter (<code>description:</code> is shown in Amethyst's list). Removing a setup file or skill from Amethyst moves it to your Amethyst archive folder by default rather than deleting it permanently.</p><h2>Multiple Claude accounts</h2><p>If you use more than one Claude subscription (for example, personal and work, or two Max/Pro plans), you can add all of them in Amethyst:</p><ul><li><strong>One-click switching:</strong> switch the active Claude account in one click. Each Amethyst window uses one Claude account at a time, so opening two Amethyst windows lets you run two Claude accounts simultaneously.</li><li><strong>Per-terminal accounts:</strong> each Claude CLI terminal you launch inside Amethyst can pick its own Claude account independently.</li><li><strong>Shared vs. separate setup:</strong> when you add an extra Claude account, Amethyst lets you choose whether it shares your main account's setup (<code>all</code>, the default) or keeps its own (<code>own</code>):</li></ul><p>  - <strong>Always shared:</strong> <code>projects</code>, <code>file-history</code>, <code>todos</code>, <code>plans</code>, and <code>history.jsonl</code> so your chat history stays together.</p><p>  - <strong>Shared in <code>all</code> mode (separate in <code>own</code> mode):</strong> <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>settings.json</code>, <code>rules</code>, <code>skills</code>, <code>plugins</code>, <code>agents</code>, <code>commands</code>, <code>output-styles</code>, and auto-memory.</p><h2>Usage and rate limits</h2><p>Amethyst displays live usage and reset times so you know when to switch accounts or pace parallel chats:</p><ul><li><strong>Claude:</strong> 5-hour window, weekly, and monthly limits per account, with reset times, alerts, and an optional button to start the 5-hour window early.</li><li><strong>Codex:</strong> rate limits and reset times from your Codex account.</li></ul><h2>Earlier chats and terminal sessions</h2><p>Chats survive closing and restarting Amethyst — they come back asleep and resume where they left off. You can also open <strong>Earlier chats</strong> to browse an agent's past sessions on disk, including Claude sessions you originally started in a standalone terminal.</p>"},"review-and-rewind":{"title":"Reviewing edits and rewind","html":"<h1>Reviewing edits and rewind</h1><p>When agents edit files in your project, Amethyst tracks every change per file and per turn so you can review diffs, keep or reject individual edits, or rewind an entire chat and its files back to an earlier point.</p><h2>The Edits strip and Review window</h2><p>At the bottom of a chat, the <strong>Edits</strong> strip shows how many files the chat has modified:</p><ul><li><strong>Expand the strip</strong> to see each changed file with individual <strong>Keep</strong> and <strong>Reject</strong> buttons.</li><li><strong>Keep all / Reject all</strong> accepts or reverts every pending file edit in the chat.</li><li><strong>Review</strong> opens the dedicated Review window showing every changed file with inline diffs, where you can:</li></ul><p>  - review changes per file or per turn,</p><p>  - keep or reject individual files,</p><p>  - undo a reject if you change your mind,</p><p>  - edit a file directly before keeping it.</p><p>You can also ask an agent to open the Review window for you at a specific file:</p><pre><code>amethyst chat review Q20 --file src/app.ts</code></pre><h2>Rewind with restore points</h2><p>Amethyst automatically records file checkpoints (restore points) before turns that edit files.</p><h3>How rewind works</h3><p>1. Trigger <strong>Rewind</strong> on any earlier message in a chat.</p><p>2. Amethyst forks the chat back to that point and shows a checklist of every file changed since then:</p><p>   - Files changed only by that chat are pre-selected to restore.</p><p>   - Files that were also modified outside the chat (by you or another agent) are grouped under <strong>check these</strong> and left unchecked by default so you do not overwrite unrelated work by accident.</p><p>3. Confirm to restore the selected files and continue from that turn.</p><p>4. If you change your mind, <strong>Undo</strong> restores both the files and the chat state.</p><h3>Storage and limits</h3><ul><li>Restore points are stored outside your repository under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Amethyst\\checkpoints</code> (Amethyst never touches your git staging area or creates extra git commits for checkpoints).</li><li>Very large files (over 50 MB) and the <code>.amethyst/</code> folder are skipped automatically.</li><li>Restore points can be turned off in <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents</strong>. When off, you can still view per-tool diffs on each tool card in the chat.</li></ul>"},"agents":{"title":"Cross-model subagents","html":"<h1>Agents from other models as subagents</h1><p>Any chat in Amethyst can start another agent kind (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>antigravity</code>, <code>cursor</code>, or <code>copilot</code>) as its own subagent. The subagent runs in the same project folder and appears under the parent chat in Agent Activity, with its own steps, answer, and short code (like <code>B73</code>).</p><h2>How to use it</h2><p>Chats started by Amethyst are not told about Amethyst's commands, so your agent will not use them on its own. Ask your agent in plain words when you want a subagent from another model, for example:</p><p>&gt; Use <code>amethyst agent run antigravity</code> with Gemini 4 Neon to review <code>src/auth.ts</code> for edge cases.</p><p>&gt; Run a Claude Opus subagent with <code>amethyst agent run claude</code> to double-check this migration.</p><p>You can also run <code>amethyst help agent</code> from a terminal to see the full command help.</p><h2>Commands</h2><p>All four commands run from inside an Amethyst chat's shell:</p><ul><li><code>amethyst agent run &lt;agent&gt; &lt;prompt&gt;</code> — start a subagent (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>antigravity</code>, <code>cursor</code>, or <code>copilot</code>) on a task and wait for its answer. Returns the answer under a header with its code (like <code>B73</code>), or returns the code immediately with <code>--background</code>.</li><li><code>amethyst agent send &lt;code&gt; &lt;text&gt;</code> — send a follow-up to one of your subagents. It keeps its earlier conversation and work.</li><li><code>amethyst agent wait &lt;codes...&gt;</code> — wait until one of the listed subagents finishes and return its answer.</li><li><code>amethyst agent stop &lt;code&gt;</code> — stop what a subagent is currently doing. It keeps what it did so far, and <code>amethyst agent send</code> can give it more work.</li></ul><h3>Flags</h3><p><code>amethyst agent run</code> accepts these flags:</p><ul><li><code>--model &lt;name&gt;</code> — pick a model the agent offers (e.g. <code>opus</code>, <code>sonnet</code>, <code>gemini-4-neon</code>, <code>gpt-5</code>).</li><li><code>--effort &lt;level&gt;</code> — pick a reasoning effort the agent offers (e.g. <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>).</li><li><code>--mode &lt;name&gt;</code> — pick a permission or collaboration mode the agent offers (e.g. <code>default</code>, <code>plan</code>). One that may work without asking, such as <code>auto</code>, needs <strong>chats answer other chats' permission asks</strong> on.</li><li><code>--worktree</code> — run the subagent in its own git worktree.</li><li><code>--background</code> — return immediately with the subagent's code so you can launch several at once and collect them with <code>amethyst agent wait</code>.</li><li><code>--timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</code> — wait at most this many seconds before returning; without it, the command waits until the subagent finishes.</li></ul><p><code>amethyst agent send</code> also accepts <code>--background</code> and <code>--timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</code>, and <code>amethyst agent wait</code> accepts <code>--timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</code>.</p><h2>Examples</h2><h3>From a Claude chat to Gemini 4 Neon (Antigravity)</h3><pre><code>amethyst agent run antigravity \"Review src/auth.ts for edge cases and list any bugs you find.\" --model gemini-4-neon --effort medium</code></pre><h3>From an Antigravity (Gemini 4 Neon) chat to Claude</h3><pre><code>amethyst agent run claude \"Review src/auth.ts for edge cases and list any bugs you find.\" --model opus --effort medium</code></pre><h2>Model names and effort</h2><p>Pass the model's family name without an effort suffix, and pass the effort separately in <code>--effort</code>:</p><ul><li>Right: <code>--model gemini-4-neon --effort medium</code></li><li>Wrong: <code>--model gemini-4-neon-medium</code> (Amethyst refuses this and replies with the list of valid model names)</li></ul><p>If you are not sure what models, efforts, or modes an agent offers, start a normal chat with that agent and run <code>amethyst chat options &lt;code&gt;</code> to list them.</p><h2>Follow-ups, parallel subagents, and stopping</h2><p>To ask a subagent a follow-up question using the code it returned:</p><pre><code>amethyst agent send B73 \"Also check how expired tokens are handled.\"</code></pre><p>To run several subagents in parallel and wait for whichever finishes first:</p><pre><code>amethyst agent run antigravity \"Check the server routes for race conditions.\" --model gemini-4-neon --effort medium --background\namethyst agent run codex \"Check the client state updates for race conditions.\" --background\namethyst agent wait B73 H57</code></pre><p>Call <code>amethyst agent wait</code> again with the remaining codes to collect the rest. To cancel a subagent mid-turn:</p><pre><code>amethyst agent stop B73</code></pre><h2>Rules and limits</h2><ul><li><strong>Chat-only:</strong> <code>amethyst agent</code> commands only work from inside a chat started by Amethyst. From your own terminal, use <code>amethyst chat new</code> to start a normal chat instead.</li><li><strong>No nesting:</strong> a subagent cannot start its own subagents.</li><li><strong>Permission asks:</strong> if a subagent asks for permission to run a command or edit a file, the prompt forwards to the parent chat for you to approve or deny. No chat or outside agent can answer it for you unless you allow it in settings.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle:</strong> cancelling or closing the parent chat stops its running subagents.</li></ul><h2>Turning it off</h2><p>To disable Amethyst's CLI tools or adjust what chats can do, open <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents -&gt; Amethyst tools</strong>.</p>"},"orchestration":{"title":"Orchestration","html":"<h1>Driving other chats (orchestration)</h1><p>Any chat in Amethyst can orchestrate other full chats in the same project: start new chats, send them tasks, wait for them to finish, read their answers, change their models, and organize them into sets or windows. You can run the exact same commands from your own terminal.</p><h2>Subagents vs. chats</h2><p>Amethyst gives you two ways to delegate work:</p><ul><li><strong>Subagents (<code>amethyst agent ...</code>):</strong> live under the calling chat in Agent Activity, are meant for focused tasks, and stop when the parent chat stops. See <strong>docs/agents.md</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chats (<code>amethyst chat ...</code>):</strong> full, visible chats in your project. You can open them, type into them, move them between sets or windows, bench them, and keep them after the orchestrator finishes.</li></ul><h2>How to ask your agent</h2><p>Chats are not given instructions about Amethyst's commands by default, so tell your agent when you want it to orchestrate other chats:</p><p>&gt; Start two chats in separate worktrees with <code>amethyst chat new --worktree</code> — one to build the API and one to write the tests — wait for both, then summarize what they did.</p><p>&gt; Read what <code>@Q20</code> just answered using <code>amethyst chat read Q20</code> and draft a follow-up in its box with <code>amethyst chat draft</code>.</p><p>Each chat has a short code like <code>Q20</code>. You can also mention another chat in your own message box by typing <code>@</code> followed by its code.</p><h2>Commands</h2><p>Run <code>amethyst help chat</code> for the live reference.</p><h3>Listing and starting chats</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst chat list</code> — list every chat in this project with its code, status (<code>starting</code>, <code>working</code>, <code>asks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>idle</code>, <code>ended</code>), agent, model, mode, title, and note.</li><li><code>amethyst chat new [text]</code> — start a new chat in this project and return its code.</li></ul><p>  - <code>--agent &lt;name&gt;</code> — <code>claude</code> (default), <code>codex</code>, <code>antigravity</code>, <code>cursor</code>, or <code>copilot</code>.</p><p>  - <code>--model &lt;name&gt;</code> — pick a model (see <code>amethyst chat options</code>).</p><p>  - <code>--mode &lt;name&gt;</code> — pick a mode (e.g. <code>default</code>, <code>plan</code>, <code>auto</code>).</p><p>  - <code>--worktree</code> — give the chat its own git worktree (created on its first message).</p><p>  - <code>--set &lt;id&gt;</code> — place it in a chat set (<code>+</code> for a new chat set), or an editor set (<code>ed:&lt;id&gt;</code> or <code>ed:new</code>).</p><p>  - <code>--attach &lt;a,b&gt;</code> — attach files (images go as images, other files as <code>@path</code>).</p><p>  - <code>--draft</code> — put the text in the chat's message box unsent so you can review and send it.</p><p>  - <code>--report</code> — turn on \"notify me\" so you get a notification each time it is ready.</p><p>  - <code>--follow</code> — jump the UI to the new chat (works from your terminal; a chat never moves your view).</p><p>  - <code>--amethyst &lt;n&gt;</code> — open it in Amethyst window <code>n</code> (1 by default).</p><h3>Sending messages and waiting</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst chat send &lt;chat&gt; &lt;text&gt;</code> — send a message to another chat. If it is busy, the message queues unless you pass <code>--now</code>. Returns a <code>qid</code> for that message.</li></ul><p>  - <code>--attach &lt;a,b&gt;</code> — attach files or images.</p><p>  - <code>--now</code> — steer into the current turn instead of queuing (for agents that support steering).</p><p>  - <code>--worktree</code> — on a fresh chat, start it in its own git worktree.</p><p>  - <code>--no-tell</code> — do not notify the calling chat automatically when the target finishes.</p><p>  - <code>--report</code> — also turn on notifications for the user.</p><ul><li><code>amethyst chat draft &lt;chat&gt; &lt;text&gt;</code> — append text to a chat's message box without sending it.</li><li><code>amethyst chat wait &lt;chats...&gt;</code> — wait until one of the listed chats finishes, asks a question, or ends. Accepts <code>--qid &lt;qid&gt;</code> and <code>--timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</code>.</li><li><code>amethyst chat read &lt;chat&gt;</code> — read the reply to a specific <code>--qid</code> or the chat's last message. Pass <code>--full</code> to include one-line tool call summaries, and <code>--from &lt;offset&gt;</code> to continue long answers.</li><li><code>amethyst chat stop &lt;chat&gt;</code> — stop the chat's current turn and clear its queued messages. The chat stays open.</li><li><code>amethyst chat end &lt;chat&gt;</code> — close a chat. A chat can only end chats that it started itself.</li></ul><h3>Questions and permission asks</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst chat asks &lt;chat&gt;</code> — list what a chat is currently asking (questions, forms, plan approvals, or permission requests) along with its options.</li><li><code>amethyst chat answer &lt;chat&gt; [option]</code> — answer a chat's question by option name, id, or number, or pass <code>cancel</code> to dismiss it.</li></ul><p>  - <code>--answers &lt;json&gt;</code> — answer a multi-field form as JSON, e.g. <code>{\"scope\":\"server\"}</code>.</p><p>  - <code>--ask &lt;id&gt;</code> — pick which ask to answer when a chat has more than one.</p><p>Permission requests (running a command, editing a file) always stay with you, in Amethyst's own window, unless you explicitly allow chats to answer them in settings. This holds for other chats, for agents outside Amethyst and for terminals, your own included. The same goes for a plan's choices that let the chat work without asking. Questions, forms, and plan choices that keep it asking can be answered by any of them.</p><h3>Models and options</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst chat options &lt;chat&gt;</code> — list a chat's available options (<code>model</code>, <code>effort</code>, <code>mode</code>, and any agent-specific options) along with their current and valid values.</li><li><code>amethyst chat set &lt;chat&gt; &lt;option&gt; &lt;value&gt;</code> — change one of the chat's options (for example, <code>amethyst chat set Q20 model sonnet</code>). Model and effort are open to every caller. With the setting below off, a mode that may work without asking (accept edits, auto, skip permissions and the like) is yours to pick in Amethyst's window, for a new chat (<code>chat new --mode</code>) and a subagent (<code>agent run --mode</code>) too.</li></ul><h3>Organizing chats and opening Review</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst chat show &lt;chat&gt;</code> — bring a chat into view in <code>--set &lt;id&gt;</code> (or next to the calling chat).</li><li><code>amethyst chat review &lt;chat&gt;</code> — open the chat's Review window for you to inspect and keep or reject its file edits. Pass <code>--file &lt;path&gt;</code> to jump straight to one file.</li><li><code>amethyst chat report &lt;chat&gt;</code> — toggle \"notify me\" notifications (<code>--on</code> or <code>--off</code>).</li><li><code>amethyst chat note &lt;chat&gt; [text]</code> — read, replace, or <code>--clear</code> the short note shown on the chat in your lists.</li><li><code>amethyst chat rename &lt;chat&gt; [title]</code> — read, set, or <code>--clear</code> a chat's custom title.</li><li><code>amethyst chat seen &lt;chat&gt;</code> — mark a finished chat as read so its unread badge clears.</li><li><code>amethyst chat move &lt;chats...&gt; --amethyst &lt;n&gt;</code> — move one or more chats to another Amethyst window while they keep running. Optional <code>--set &lt;id&gt;</code>.</li></ul><h2>Scope and safety</h2><ul><li><strong>Same project only:</strong> a chat can only list, read, or drive chats in its own project.</li><li><strong>No self-calls:</strong> a chat cannot send messages to itself, wait for itself, or end itself.</li><li><strong>Turn limit:</strong> a chat can send at most 50 messages per turn before it must finish its turn or ask you.</li></ul><h2>Settings</h2><p>Open <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents -&gt; Amethyst tools</strong> to control orchestration:</p><ul><li><strong>chats can drive other chats</strong> — turns chat orchestration and <code>@</code> chat mentions on or off.</li><li><strong>chats answer other chats' permission asks</strong> — off by default; when off, permission prompts always wait for you, whoever tries to answer them: another chat, an agent outside Amethyst, a script or a terminal. They can't turn this setting on or pick a mode that doesn't ask either, and a message they send to a chat you put in such a mode shows in that chat as a permission ask: Allow sends it, Don't allow drops it. While it's off, Amethyst also asks you before it runs a project's code for them: an Unreal build, project files, Unreal Editor or debugging, a git action in a repo with hooks that would run, and an evaluate with a call or an assignment in the debugged program. And they can't clone, pull, switch or check out anything that brings in a repo's files that let chats skip asking (an agent's settings or hooks): you do that from Amethyst's window.</li></ul>"},"cli-and-mcp":{"title":"CLI and MCP server","html":"<h1>CLI and MCP server</h1><p>Amethyst exposes the same set of actions in two ways:</p><p>1. The <strong><code>amethyst</code> CLI</strong> for your own terminals, scripts, and chats running inside Amethyst.</p><p>2. An <strong>MCP server</strong> (<code>amethyst mcp</code>) for agents running outside Amethyst (such as Claude Code in a standalone terminal, Codex CLI, or other MCP clients).</p><p>Both talk to the running Amethyst app over its local server, so they always stay in sync.</p><h2>Using the <code>amethyst</code> CLI</h2><p>When you install Amethyst on Windows, it places <code>amethyst.cmd</code> in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Amethyst\\bin</code> and adds that folder to your user <code>PATH</code>. It uses Amethyst's own runtime, so you do not need a separate Node.js installation.</p><p>Open a new terminal and run:</p><pre><code>amethyst help\namethyst help chat\namethyst help agent\namethyst help git\namethyst help pr\namethyst help issue\namethyst help debug</code></pre><p>For the full list of every command, flag, and MCP tool name, see the auto-generated <strong>Command and MCP reference</strong>.</p><h3>Global flags</h3><p>Every <code>amethyst &lt;feature&gt; &lt;action&gt;</code> command accepts these flags:</p><ul><li><code>--json</code> — print the full structured JSON response instead of human-readable text.</li><li><code>--project &lt;name&gt;</code> — target a named Amethyst project instead of detecting the project from your current folder.</li><li><code>--am &lt;n&gt;</code> — target Amethyst window number <code>n</code>.</li></ul><h3>Exit codes</h3><ul><li><code>0</code> — succeeded.</li><li><code>1</code> — failed (Amethyst returned an error, or Amethyst is not running).</li><li><code>2</code> — invalid command or arguments.</li></ul><h3>How Amethyst finds the running app</h3><p>Amethyst writes its local port to <code>%APPDATA%\\Amethyst\\.amethyst\\server.json</code>. If you are running a separate dev instance, set <code>AMETHYST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code> in your environment to point the CLI at it.</p><h2>How chats inside Amethyst use it</h2><p>By default, Amethyst adds no extra instructions to your chats so your context window stays clean.</p><ul><li><strong>Claude, Codex, Cursor, and GitHub Copilot chats</strong> inside Amethyst get <code>amethyst</code> on their shell <code>PATH</code> along with their chat identity (<code>AMETHYST_URL</code>, <code>AMETHYST_CHAT</code>, <code>AMETHYST_PASS</code>). When you ask them to use an <code>amethyst</code> command, they run it in their shell.</li><li><strong>Antigravity chats</strong> inside Amethyst also receive the <code>amethyst</code> MCP server automatically.</li></ul><p>Subagents started with <code>amethyst agent run</code> do not receive the CLI identity or MCP server, so they cannot spawn nested subagents.</p><h2>Connecting outside agents with MCP</h2><p>If you want an agent running outside Amethyst to drive your Amethyst chats, open diffs in the UI, or use the Unreal debugger, run:</p><pre><code>amethyst mcp add</code></pre><p>It prints ready-to-paste setup snippets for your platform:</p><h3>Claude Code</h3><pre><code>claude mcp add amethyst -- cmd /c amethyst mcp</code></pre><h3>Codex (<code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>)</h3><pre><code>[mcp_servers.amethyst]\ncommand = \"cmd\"\nargs = [\"/c\", \"amethyst\", \"mcp\"]</code></pre><h3>Other MCP clients (JSON)</h3><pre><code>{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"amethyst\": {\n      \"command\": \"cmd\",\n      \"args\": [\"/c\", \"amethyst\", \"mcp\"]\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre><p>The outside agent acts on the Amethyst project that owns the folder it runs in.</p><h3>What an outside agent or a terminal can't do</h3><p>Amethyst counts only its own window as you. Any other caller (an agent over MCP, the <code>amethyst</code> command in any terminal, a script that calls the local address) reaches just the commands listed here. Unless you turn on <strong>chats answer other chats' permission asks</strong>, it can't answer a permission ask, approve a plan so that the chat works without asking, or pick a mode that doesn't ask, and what it sends to a chat you put in such a mode waits in that chat as a permission ask until you allow it. When it builds, opens or debugs an Unreal project, runs a git action in a repo with hooks, or evaluates a call in the program you debug, Amethyst asks you first in its window (Allow or not now), every time. A clone, pull, sync, branch switch, <code>pr checkout</code> or <code>issue start</code> that would bring in an agent's settings or hooks (<code>.claude/settings.local.json</code>, <code>.mcp.json</code>, <code>.codex/</code>, <code>.cursor/cli.json</code>, <code>.github/hooks/</code> and the like, also inside a submodule) is refused: those files can let chats run commands without asking you, so that change is yours to make from Amethyst's window. It can never change Amethyst's settings or open a terminal in it. Answer permission asks in the app.</p><h3>CLI-only commands vs. MCP tools</h3><p>Most <code>chat</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>debug</code>, and UI-opening <code>git</code>/<code>pr</code>/<code>issue</code> actions are available in both the CLI and MCP (where <code>amethyst chat read</code> becomes the tool <code>chat_read</code>). Routine git and GitHub write commands like <code>git commit</code>, <code>git push</code>, <code>git branch</code>, and <code>pr create</code> are CLI-only, because coding agents already run <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code> directly.</p><h2>Settings</h2><p>Open <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents -&gt; Amethyst tools</strong> to turn <strong>the amethyst command in terminals</strong> on or off (this adds or removes <code>amethyst.cmd</code> from your <code>PATH</code>).</p>"},"commands":{"title":"Command and MCP reference","html":"<h1>Command and MCP reference</h1><p>This reference is generated directly from Amethyst's action tables so every command, flag, and MCP tool name stays in sync with the app.</p><p>Run <code>amethyst help</code> or <code>amethyst help &lt;feature&gt;</code> in your terminal for the same help text from the running app. For setup and workflows, see <strong>CLI and MCP server</strong>.</p><h2>Global flags and exit codes</h2><p>Every <code>amethyst &lt;feature&gt; &lt;action&gt;</code> command accepts:</p><ul><li><code>--json</code> — print the full JSON response.</li><li><code>--project &lt;name&gt;</code> — target a named Amethyst project instead of detecting from the current folder.</li><li><code>--am &lt;n&gt;</code> — target Amethyst window number <code>n</code>.</li></ul><p>Exit codes: <code>0</code> done, <code>1</code> failed (error from Amethyst or app not running), <code>2</code> invalid usage.</p><h2>Features</h2><ul><li><strong>Agent subagents (<code>amethyst agent</code>)</strong></li><li><strong>Chat orchestration (<code>amethyst chat</code>)</strong></li><li><strong>Git (<code>amethyst git</code>)</strong></li><li><strong>Pull requests (<code>amethyst pr</code>)</strong></li><li><strong>Issues (<code>amethyst issue</code>)</strong></li><li><strong>Unreal debugger (<code>amethyst debug</code>)</strong></li></ul><h2>Agent subagents (<code>amethyst agent</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst agent run</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>agent_run</code></p><p>Start a subagent of any kind (claude, codex, antigravity, cursor or copilot) on a task and get its answer. It runs as its own agent in this project's folder; the user sees it as a subagent of this chat, with its steps. Returns its answer under a first line naming it by its code (like \"Codex - H57:\"), the code amethyst agent send takes; with background, its code at once. A mode that may work without asking is the user's to pick: a chat can't start one in it unless the user allowed chats to answer permission asks.</p><pre><code>amethyst agent run &lt;agent&gt; &lt;prompt...&gt; [--model &lt;model&gt;] [--effort &lt;effort&gt;] [--mode &lt;mode&gt;] [--worktree] [--background] [--timeout &lt;timeout&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>agent</code> (string, required): claude, codex, antigravity, cursor or copilot</li><li><code>prompt</code> (string, required): The task, as you would give it to a person who knows nothing of this chat</li><li><code>--model</code> (string, optional): A model it offers, e.g. opus, sonnet or gpt-5</li><li><code>--effort</code> (string, optional): An effort it offers, e.g. low, medium or high</li><li><code>--mode</code> (string, optional): A mode it offers that asks, e.g. default or plan</li><li><code>--worktree</code> (boolean, optional): Work in its own git worktree</li><li><code>--background</code> (boolean, optional): Return at once; you are told when it finishes, and amethyst agent wait gives its answer</li><li><code>--timeout</code> (number, optional): Seconds to wait at most; without it, it waits until it is done</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst agent send</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>agent_send</code></p><p>Send one of your subagents a follow-up; it keeps what it did before. Returns its answer, or with background at once.</p><pre><code>amethyst agent send &lt;agent&gt; &lt;text...&gt; [--background] [--timeout &lt;timeout&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>agent</code> (string, required): The subagent: the code amethyst agent run gave it (like S71)</li><li><code>text</code> (string, required): The follow-up</li><li><code>--background</code> (boolean, optional): Return at once; amethyst agent wait gives its answer</li><li><code>--timeout</code> (number, optional): Seconds to wait at most; without it, it waits until it is done</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst agent wait</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>agent_wait</code></p><p>Wait until one of these subagents finishes, and get its answer. With a timeout, when it runs out it says which still work.</p><pre><code>amethyst agent wait &lt;agents...&gt; [--timeout &lt;timeout&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>agents</code> (list, required): Their codes</li><li><code>--timeout</code> (number, optional): Seconds to wait at most; without it, it waits until it is done</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst agent stop</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>agent_stop</code></p><p>Stop what one of your subagents is doing. It keeps what it did; amethyst agent send gives it more.</p><pre><code>amethyst agent stop &lt;agent&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>agent</code> (string, required): The subagent: the code amethyst agent run gave it (like S71)</li></ul><h2>Chat orchestration (<code>amethyst chat</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst chat list</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_list</code></p><p>The chats in this project: each one's code (Q20), title, agent, model, mode and status (starting, working, asks, done, idle, ended), and since when.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat list</code></pre><h3><code>amethyst chat new</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_new</code></p><p>Start a new chat in this project. It is a normal chat the user sees and can type into; it goes next to yours when your set has a free cell, else in the set given, else in the agents list. With text it gets that as its first message (or, with draft, finds it in its message box for the user to send). Returns its code. Give it more work with amethyst chat send. A mode that may work without asking is the user's to pick: a chat, an agent outside Amethyst or a terminal can't start one in it unless the user allowed chats to answer permission asks.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat new [text...] [--agent &lt;agent&gt;] [--model &lt;model&gt;] [--mode &lt;mode&gt;] [--worktree] [--set &lt;set&gt;] [--report] [--attach &lt;a,b&gt;] [--draft] [--follow] [--amethyst &lt;amethyst&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--agent</code> (string, optional): claude (the default), codex, antigravity, cursor or copilot</li><li><code>--model</code> (string, optional): A model it offers, e.g. opus or sonnet (amethyst chat options lists them)</li><li><code>--mode</code> (string, optional): A mode it offers that asks, e.g. default or plan</li><li><code>--worktree</code> (boolean, optional): Work in its own git worktree (made with its first message)</li><li><code>--set</code> (string, optional): Where to show it: a chat set (its id, or + for a new one), or an editor set (ed:&lt;id&gt;, or ed:new for a new one)</li><li><code>--report</code> (boolean, optional): Notify me: the user gets a notification each time it is ready (amethyst chat report turns it off)</li><li><code>text</code> (string, optional): Its first message, sent at once</li><li><code>--attach</code> (list, optional): Files to send with the first message: pictures go as pictures, other files as @path</li><li><code>--draft</code> (boolean, optional): Put the text in its message box unsent, for the user to send</li><li><code>--follow</code> (boolean, optional): Take the user to it (not from a chat: a chat never moves the user)</li><li><code>--amethyst</code> (number, optional): The Amethyst (window) it opens in, 1 by default; it shows there when that Amethyst opens</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat new --set + --follow \"plan the landing page\"</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat send</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_send</code></p><p>Send a message to another chat, as if the user typed it (it shows as from you). If the chat is busy, the message waits in its queue. Returns the message's qid. When it finishes, you get a short message saying so (unless tell is false), or call amethyst chat wait; then amethyst chat read gives the answer.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat send &lt;chat&gt; &lt;text...&gt; [--attach &lt;a,b&gt;] [--now] [--worktree] [--tell] [--report]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>text</code> (string, required): The message</li><li><code>--attach</code> (list, optional): Files to attach: pictures go as pictures, other files as @path</li><li><code>--now</code> (boolean, optional): If the chat is busy, put the message into its current turn instead of the queue (agents that can take it)</li><li><code>--worktree</code> (boolean, optional): On a fresh chat: work in its own git worktree</li><li><code>--tell</code> (boolean, optional): false: no message to you when it finishes (default true)</li><li><code>--report</code> (boolean, optional): Also turn on notify me for the chat: the user gets a notification each time it is ready</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat send Q20 \"write a haiku\"</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat draft</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_draft</code></p><p>Put text in a chat's message box without sending it: the user reads it, changes it if they like, and sends it. Text already in the box stays; this goes after it.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat draft &lt;chat&gt; &lt;text...&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>text</code> (string, required): The text</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat draft Q20 \"also check the footer\"</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat stop</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_stop</code></p><p>Stop the chat's current turn and take back its queued messages. The chat stays open.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat stop &lt;chat&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat seen</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_seen</code></p><p>Mark a finished chat as read: its green mark and finished badge go, and the counts drop. A chat that isn't finished and unread is left as it is.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat seen &lt;chat&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat wait</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_wait</code></p><p>Wait until one of these chats finishes the message you sent it, asks something, or ends; it returns that one. A chat you sent nothing to returns when it stops working. With a timeout, when it runs out it says which are still working.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat wait &lt;chats...&gt; [--qid &lt;qid&gt;] [--timeout &lt;timeout&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chats</code> (list, required): The chats to wait for: codes, titles or ids</li><li><code>--qid</code> (string, optional): Wait for this message's answer only</li><li><code>--timeout</code> (number, optional): Seconds to wait at most; without it, it waits until it is done</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat wait Q20 V10</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat read</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_read</code></p><p>A chat's answer: the reply to your message (qid), or to its last message. With full, the tools it ran too, one line each. Long answers come in parts: from says where to go on.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat read &lt;chat&gt; [--qid &lt;qid&gt;] [--full] [--from &lt;from&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>--qid</code> (string, optional): The message whose answer to read (default: the last one)</li><li><code>--full</code> (boolean, optional): Also the tools it ran, one line each</li><li><code>--from</code> (number, optional): Go on from this character</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat asks</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_asks</code></p><p>What the chat asks now: questions, forms, plan approvals and permission asks, each with its options.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat asks &lt;chat&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat answer</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_answer</code></p><p>Answer what the chat asks: an option (its name, id or number), or, for a form with several questions, answers by field. Permission asks (run a command, edit a file), and a plan's choices that let the chat work without asking, are the user's to answer in Amethyst's window. That holds for chats, agents outside Amethyst and terminals, unless the user allowed chats to answer permission asks.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat answer &lt;chat&gt; [option...] [--answers &lt;json&gt;] [--ask &lt;ask&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>option</code> (string, optional): The option or the answer text; cancel to dismiss it</li><li><code>--answers</code> (object, optional): For a form: { field: value } (amethyst chat asks lists the fields)</li><li><code>--ask</code> (string, optional): Which ask, when it has several (amethyst chat asks lists them)</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat options</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_options</code></p><p>The chat's options and what each is set to: model, effort, mode, Ultracode and whatever its agent offers, with the values each takes.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat options &lt;chat&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat set</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_set</code></p><p>Set one of the chat's options, as the user would in its box: model, effort, mode, Ultracode (on or off), ... (amethyst chat options lists them). Model, effort and the modes that ask are open to all; a mode that may work without asking is the user's to pick, also from agents outside Amethyst and terminals, unless the user allowed chats to answer permission asks.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat set &lt;chat&gt; &lt;option&gt; &lt;value&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>option</code> (string, required): The option: its id or name</li><li><code>value</code> (string, required): The value: its id or name</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat set Q20 model sonnet</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat show</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_show</code></p><p>Show the chat to the user: in the set given, or next to the calling chat when its set has a free cell, or in the chat set that's on.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat show &lt;chat&gt; [--set &lt;set&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>--set</code> (string, optional): A chat set (its id, or + for a new one), or an editor set (ed:&lt;id&gt;, or ed:new)</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst chat review</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_review</code></p><p>Open the chat's Review window for the user: every file it changed, with the changes marked and Keep / Reject on each. With a file, the window opens at that file.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat review &lt;chat&gt; [--file &lt;file&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>--file</code> (string, optional): A file the chat changed: its path in the project</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat review Q20 --file src/app.ts</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat report</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_report</code></p><p>Notify me: while it's on, the user gets a notification each time the chat is ready (its turn ended, or it asks something) and they aren't looking at it. The notification shows the start of the answer and takes them to the chat. With no on or off it flips.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat report &lt;chat&gt; [--on] [--off]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>--on</code> (boolean, optional): true: turn it on; false: turn it off</li><li><code>--off</code> (boolean, optional): Turn it off</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat report Q20 --off</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat note</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_note</code></p><p>The chat's note: a short text the user (or you) leaves on a chat, such as what it waits for. It shows in the user's chat lists. With text it replaces the note; with clear it removes it; with neither it returns the note.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat note &lt;chat&gt; [text...] [--clear]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>text</code> (string, optional): The new note</li><li><code>--clear</code> (boolean, optional): Remove the note</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat note Q20 waiting for 850 to finish</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat rename</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_rename</code></p><p>Rename a chat: the name the user sees for it in their chat lists and its header. With a title it renames it; with clear it gets its automatic name back (the agent's own title, or the start of its first message); with neither it returns the name.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat rename &lt;chat&gt; [title...] [--clear]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li><li><code>title</code> (string, optional): The new name</li><li><code>--clear</code> (boolean, optional): Give it its automatic name back</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat rename Q20 plan for the importer</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat move</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_move</code></p><p>Move chats to another Amethyst (a window of the app, numbered from 1), into a set there. They keep running; that Amethyst shows them, this one doesn't.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat move &lt;chats...&gt; [--amethyst &lt;amethyst&gt;] [--set &lt;set&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>chats</code> (list, required): The chats to move: codes, titles or ids</li><li><code>--amethyst</code> (number, required): The number of the Amethyst to move them to</li><li><code>--set</code> (string, optional): Where there: a chat set (its id, or + for one new set for all of them), or an editor set (ed:&lt;id&gt;, or ed:new). Default: the set that's on there</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst chat move Q20 V10 --amethyst 1</code></p><h3><code>amethyst chat end</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>chat_end</code></p><p>End a chat and close it. A chat can end only the chats it started.</p><pre><code>amethyst chat end &lt;chat&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>chat</code> (string, required): The chat: its code (Q20 or @Q20), its title, or its id. Only chats in your own project.</li></ul><h2>Git (<code>amethyst git</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst git status</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>git_status</code></p><p>What source control shows for this project: the branch and its remote branch, commits to push and to pull, a merge or rebase under way, and each changed file with its letter (M changed, A new, D deleted, R renamed, C conflict) and whether it is staged.</p><pre><code>amethyst git status [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git repos</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>The repositories in this project: its own, its submodules, and folders one level down that are repositories.</p><pre><code>amethyst git repos</code></pre><h3><code>amethyst git commit</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Commit the changed files, as the commit button does: what is staged in git if anything is, otherwise every change (secrets left out). Prints what was committed.</p><pre><code>amethyst git commit [message...] [--push] [--amend] [--force] [--skip &lt;a,b&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>message</code> (string, optional): The commit message</li><li><code>--push</code> (boolean, optional): Push after the commit</li><li><code>--amend</code> (boolean, optional): Change the last commit instead of making a new one: what is staged goes into it (nothing staged: only the message changes)</li><li><code>--force</code> (boolean, optional): With amend: also when the last commit is on the remote already</li><li><code>--skip</code> (list, optional): Files to leave out</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git commit \"Fix the login form\" --push</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git fetch</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Ask the remote for new commits now. Prints how many there are to pull and to push.</p><pre><code>amethyst git fetch [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git pull</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Pull the remote’s commits into this branch. When both sides have new commits it follows git’s pull.rebase setting, or --how.</p><pre><code>amethyst git pull [--how &lt;how&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--how</code> (string, optional): rebase or merge, when both sides have new commits</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git pull --how rebase</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git push</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Push this branch; the first time, publish it.</p><pre><code>amethyst git push [--lease] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--lease</code> (boolean, optional): Push over the remote’s old version of commits changed here (force with lease)</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git sync</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Pull, then push.</p><pre><code>amethyst git sync [--how &lt;how&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--how</code> (string, optional): rebase or merge, when both sides have new commits</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git branch</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Switch to a branch, or make one with --create.</p><pre><code>amethyst git branch &lt;name&gt; [--create] [--from &lt;from&gt;] [--changes &lt;changes&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>name</code> (string, required): The branch</li><li><code>--create</code> (boolean, optional): Make it and switch to it; the changes come along</li><li><code>--from</code> (string, optional): With create: the branch, tag or commit it starts at</li><li><code>--changes</code> (string, optional): stash: leave the changed files on the branch you leave; they come back when you switch back</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git branch fix-login --create --from origin/main</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git branch-names</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>git_branch_names</code></p><p>The patterns this project names new branches by, with an example of each: chat (a chat's own worktree, default chat/{chat}), issue (starting on an issue, default {n}-{title}), pr (a pull request in its own folder, default pr/{n}) and new (what the new branch box starts with, default empty). Placeholders: {chat} the chat's code, {n} the issue or pull request number, {title} the issue title, {user} your git user name. Give a pattern to change it, an empty one for its default; they apply to branches made from then on.</p><pre><code>amethyst git branch-names [--chat &lt;chat&gt;] [--issue &lt;issue&gt;] [--pr &lt;pr&gt;] [--new &lt;new&gt;] [--reset]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--chat</code> (string, optional): A chat's worktree branch, e.g. ai/{chat}</li><li><code>--issue</code> (string, optional): A branch for an issue; needs {n}, e.g. feature/{n}-{title}</li><li><code>--pr</code> (string, optional): A pull request opened in its own folder; needs {n}, e.g. review/{n}</li><li><code>--new</code> (string, optional): What the new branch box starts with, e.g. {user}/</li><li><code>--reset</code> (boolean, optional): All four back to their defaults</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git branch-names --issue \"feature/{n}-{title}\" --chat \"ai/{chat}\"</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git abort</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Undo the merge or rebase that is under way.</p><pre><code>amethyst git abort [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git skip</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Leave out the commit that clashes and go on with the rebase, cherry-pick or revert under way.</p><pre><code>amethyst git skip [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git clone</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Copy a repository onto this PC, with its submodules, and add it as a project to the workspace it is run from (a chat or terminal in one of its projects). Run outside a workspace, it only copies. Prints where it went.</p><pre><code>amethyst git clone &lt;url&gt; [into] [--name &lt;name&gt;] [--add]</code></pre><ul><li><code>url</code> (string, required): Its address, or owner/repo for GitHub</li><li><code>into</code> (string, optional): The folder (new or empty); by default the repository’s name, here</li><li><code>--name</code> (string, optional): The project’s name in Amethyst</li><li><code>--add</code> (boolean, optional): Add it as a project (default true)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git clone owner/repo</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git tag</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Tag the commit you are on (or --at another), and with --push send the tag to the remote.</p><pre><code>amethyst git tag &lt;name&gt; [--at &lt;at&gt;] [--message &lt;message&gt;] [--push] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>name</code> (string, required): The tag, usually a version: v1.2.0</li><li><code>--at</code> (string, optional): A branch, tag or commit to tag instead</li><li><code>--message</code> (string, optional): Makes an annotated tag with this message</li><li><code>--push</code> (boolean, optional): Push the tag</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git tag v1.2.0 --push</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git log</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>The commits of this branch, newest first: hash, date, author and subject, marked when not pushed. Fifty at a time.</p><pre><code>amethyst git log [path] [--q &lt;q&gt;] [--ref &lt;ref&gt;] [--skip &lt;skip&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>path</code> (string, optional): Only the commits that changed this file, past its renames</li><li><code>--q</code> (string, optional): Search: words in the message, author:name, or code:text for when a text came in or went</li><li><code>--ref</code> (string, optional): Another branch, tag or commit</li><li><code>--skip</code> (number, optional): Skip this many, for the next page</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git log --q author:ana</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git blame</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Who changed each line of a file: line number, commit, author and the line.</p><pre><code>amethyst git blame &lt;path&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>path</code> (string, required): The file</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git diff</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>git_show_diff</code></p><p>Open a file's changes in Amethyst's diff tab for the user: the last commit against the disk, or --a and --b (commits, branches or tags; b defaults to the disk).</p><pre><code>amethyst git diff &lt;path&gt; [--a &lt;a&gt;] [--b &lt;b&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>path</code> (string, required): The file</li><li><code>--a</code> (string, optional): The older side</li><li><code>--b</code> (string, optional): The newer side</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git diff src/app.ts --a main</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git history</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>git_show_history</code></p><p>Open the history in Amethyst's source control panel for the user: the branch's, another ref's, or one file's.</p><pre><code>amethyst git history [path] [--ref &lt;ref&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>path</code> (string, optional): Only this file</li><li><code>--ref</code> (string, optional): Another branch, tag or commit</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git compare</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>git_show_branch</code></p><p>Open a branch in Amethyst for the user: its commits and files against the base (the remote's main branch unless --base), each opening as a diff.</p><pre><code>amethyst git compare &lt;ref&gt; [--base &lt;base&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>ref</code> (string, required): The branch</li><li><code>--base</code> (string, optional): What to compare it with</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git compare chat/q20</code></p><h3><code>amethyst git continue</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Go on with the merge or rebase that is under way, once its conflicts are fixed.</p><pre><code>amethyst git continue [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder (amethyst git repos lists them); the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst git release</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Publish a GitHub release for a tag (pushed first if needed), with notes GitHub writes from the pull requests since the last one, or your own.</p><pre><code>amethyst git release &lt;tag&gt; [--title &lt;title&gt;] [--notes &lt;notes&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>tag</code> (string, required): The tag, e.g. v1.2.0</li><li><code>--title</code> (string, optional): Its title; the tag by default</li><li><code>--notes</code> (string, optional): Your own notes instead</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst git release v1.2.0</code></p><h2>Pull requests (<code>amethyst pr</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst pr list</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>pr_list</code></p><p>The open pull requests of this project's GitHub repository: number, title, author, branch → base, draft, review state and checks.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr list [--show &lt;show&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--show</code> (string, optional): open (the default), mine, or review (waiting for my review)</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst pr list --show review</code></p><h3><code>amethyst pr view</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>pr_view</code></p><p>One pull request: its state, branch → base, files and lines, review state, each check, what blocks the merge, and its description.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr view &lt;n&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst pr open</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>pr_open</code></p><p>Open a pull request in its tab in Amethyst, for the user.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr open &lt;n&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst pr create</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Open a pull request for the branch you are on (pushed first if needed); an issue number in the branch name is linked with \"Fixes #N\".</p><pre><code>amethyst pr create &lt;title...&gt; [--body &lt;body&gt;] [--base &lt;base&gt;] [--draft] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>title</code> (string, required): Its title</li><li><code>--body</code> (string, optional): Its description, markdown</li><li><code>--base</code> (string, optional): The branch it goes into; the repository’s main one by default</li><li><code>--draft</code> (boolean, optional): Open it as a draft</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst pr create \"Fix the login\" --draft</code></p><h3><code>amethyst pr comment</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Comment on a pull request.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr comment &lt;n&gt; &lt;body...&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>body</code> (string, required): The comment</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst pr review</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Review a pull request: approve, ask for changes, or comment.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr review &lt;n&gt; [--how &lt;how&gt;] [--body &lt;body&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>--how</code> (string, required): approve, changes or comment</li><li><code>--body</code> (string, optional): What the review says (needed to ask for changes)</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst pr review 12 --how approve</code></p><h3><code>amethyst pr merge</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Merge a pull request on GitHub, in a way the repository allows.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr merge &lt;n&gt; [--how &lt;how&gt;] [--delete-branch] [--auto] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>--how</code> (string, required): squash, merge or rebase</li><li><code>--delete-branch</code> (boolean, optional): Delete its branch after</li><li><code>--auto</code> (boolean, optional): Merge by itself once what blocks it is done</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst pr merge 12 --how squash</code></p><h3><code>amethyst pr checkout</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Switch the project's folder to a pull request's branch, or with --worktree open it in a folder of its own.</p><pre><code>amethyst pr checkout &lt;n&gt; [--worktree] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The pull request’s number</li><li><code>--worktree</code> (boolean, optional): In its own folder</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h2>Issues (<code>amethyst issue</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst issue list</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>issue_list</code></p><p>The issues of this project's GitHub repository: number, title, labels, author, comments.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue list [--show &lt;show&gt;] [--q &lt;q&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--show</code> (string, optional): open (the default), mine (assigned to me) or closed</li><li><code>--q</code> (string, optional): Search words</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst issue list --show mine</code></p><h3><code>amethyst issue view</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>issue_view</code></p><p>One issue: its state, labels, assignees, the pull requests that close it, its text and its comments.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue view &lt;n&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The issue’s number</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst issue open</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>issue_open</code></p><p>Open an issue in its tab in Amethyst, for the user.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue open &lt;n&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The issue’s number</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst issue new</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Open a new issue.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue new &lt;title...&gt; [--body &lt;body&gt;] [--labels &lt;a,b&gt;] [--me] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>title</code> (string, required): Its title</li><li><code>--body</code> (string, optional): Its text, markdown</li><li><code>--labels</code> (list, optional): Labels</li><li><code>--me</code> (boolean, optional): Assign it to me</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst issue new \"Crash on start\" --labels bug --me</code></p><h3><code>amethyst issue comment</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Comment on an issue.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue comment &lt;n&gt; &lt;body...&gt; [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The issue’s number</li><li><code>body</code> (string, required): The comment</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst issue close</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Close an issue, as done or as not planned.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue close &lt;n&gt; [--reason &lt;reason&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The issue’s number</li><li><code>--reason</code> (string, optional): completed (the default) or \"not planned\"</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst issue start</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI only</p><p>Start on an issue: a branch named for it, linked to it on GitHub, and this folder switched to it; with --where folder, in a folder of its own instead.</p><pre><code>amethyst issue start &lt;n&gt; [--where &lt;where&gt;] [--repo &lt;repo&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>n</code> (number, required): The issue’s number</li><li><code>--where</code> (string, optional): here (the default) or folder</li><li><code>--repo</code> (string, optional): A repository inside the project, by its folder; the project’s own when left out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst issue start 12</code></p><h2>Unreal debugger (<code>amethyst debug</code>)</h2><h3><code>amethyst debug start</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_start</code></p><p>Start debugging this project's Unreal program: Unreal Editor (DebugGame Editor), built first, then started under the debugger; when the editor is already open it attaches to it instead. With game: the game in its own window (Unreal Editor with -game). With attach: only attach to the open editor. It returns at once; set breakpoints with amethyst debug break, then amethyst debug wait returns at the first stop.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug start [--game] [--attach] [--build]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--game</code> (boolean, optional): Debug the game: Unreal Editor with -game, in a window</li><li><code>--attach</code> (boolean, optional): Attach to the Unreal Editor that is open on this project</li><li><code>--build</code> (boolean, optional): Build the Editor target first (default true)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug start --game</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug status</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_status</code></p><p>The debugger in this project: not debugging, building, running, or stopped, and then why (a breakpoint, a step, a crash, check() or ensure() failed, a value changed), where (file:line, function and the line's code), and whether an agent did it. Also how many breakpoints are set.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug status</code></pre><h3><code>amethyst debug stop</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_stop</code></p><p>Stop debugging. By default it detaches: Unreal Editor (or the game) keeps running, with its unsaved work. With end: true it ends the program instead, and unsaved work in Unreal Editor is lost: only do that when the user asked for it.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug stop [--end]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--end</code> (boolean, optional): End the program instead of detaching: unsaved work in Unreal Editor is lost</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug stop</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug continue</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_continue</code></p><p>Let the stopped program run on. It returns at once; amethyst debug wait returns at the next stop.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug continue</code></pre><h3><code>amethyst debug pause</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_pause</code></p><p>Stop the running program where it is (on Unreal's GameThread when the pause lands elsewhere). Returns where it stopped.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug pause</code></pre><h3><code>amethyst debug step</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_step</code></p><p>Step the stopped program one line: over (the default) runs calls on this line without stopping in them, into goes into the call, out runs to the end of this function. Returns where it stopped next.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug step [how]</code></pre><ul><li><code>how</code> (string, optional): over (the default), into or out</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug step into</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug break</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_breakpoints</code></p><p>Breakpoints in this project. With at: set one at file:line (a path in the project, or just the file's name when it's the only one), with if: only when that C++ condition is true. With remove: take the one at file:line away. With neither: list them, each with whether it's bound (or waiting for its module to load), its hits and who set it. The program stops there next time the line runs.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug break [at] [--if &lt;if&gt;] [--remove &lt;remove&gt;] [--list]</code></pre><ul><li><code>at</code> (string, optional): Set a breakpoint at file:line, e.g. Source/Game/Pix.cpp:10 or Pix.cpp:10</li><li><code>--if</code> (string, optional): A C++ condition: stop only when it is true, e.g. Health &lt; 0</li><li><code>--remove</code> (string, optional): Remove the breakpoint at file:line</li><li><code>--list</code> (boolean, optional): Only list them (the default with no at or remove)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug break Pix.cpp:10 --if \"Health &lt; 0\"</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug stack</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_stack</code></p><p>The call stack where the program stopped, one frame a line: #N function  file:line. Engine frames are marked (engine) and long runs of them fold into one line unless all is true; Blueprint frames show as [Blueprint] BP_Name - Function (the object). Use the numbers with amethyst debug vars and amethyst debug eval.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug stack [--all] [--thread &lt;thread&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--all</code> (boolean, optional): Every engine frame, not folded</li><li><code>--thread</code> (number, optional): Another thread by its id (default: the one that stopped)</li></ul><h3><code>amethyst debug vars</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_variables</code></p><p>The variables of a frame while stopped (the locals, with this), as an indented tree: name = value - type, Unreal types read as in Visual Studio (FString as its text, TArray as Num=N with its items, UObject as Name (Class)). {...} means it opens further with a larger depth.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug vars [--frame &lt;frame&gt;] [--depth &lt;depth&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--frame</code> (number, optional): The frame: its number in amethyst debug stack (#0 is where it stopped, the default)</li><li><code>--depth</code> (number, optional): How many levels to open, 1 to 4 (default 2)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug vars --frame 1 --depth 3</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug eval</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_evaluate</code></p><p>Evaluate a C++ expression in a frame while stopped, e.g. Health, this-&gt;Weapon or Items.Num. Returns its value and type, and its members when it has some. An expression with a call or an assignment could change the program: that needs the user's \"agents can control the debugger\".</p><pre><code>amethyst debug eval &lt;expr...&gt; [--frame &lt;frame&gt;] [--depth &lt;depth&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>expr</code> (string, required): The expression</li><li><code>--frame</code> (number, optional): The frame: its number in amethyst debug stack (#0 is where it stopped, the default)</li><li><code>--depth</code> (number, optional): How many levels of its members to show, 0 to 3 (default 0: one level)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug eval this-&gt;Health</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug wait</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_wait</code></p><p>Wait until the program stops (a breakpoint, a step, a crash) or debugging ends, then say where and why, as amethyst debug status does. Returns at once if it is stopped already. After ms (default 30000, at most 900000) it says it is still running: call it again to keep waiting.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug wait [--ms &lt;ms&gt;]</code></pre><ul><li><code>--ms</code> (number, optional): How long to wait, in milliseconds (default 30000)</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug wait --ms 60000</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug goto</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_goto</code></p><p>Set the next statement: while stopped, move the program to another line of the same function (file:line). Code skipped or run again is not undone. The adapter refuses a line outside the function, and says why.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug goto &lt;at&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>at</code> (string, required): The line to run next: file:line, in the function where it stopped</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug goto Pix.cpp:12</code></p><h3><code>amethyst debug runto</code></h3><p><strong>Surface:</strong> CLI and MCP tool <code>debug_run_to</code></p><p>Run until the program reaches file:line (a breakpoint there for this once only, never saved), or stops anywhere else first. It returns at once; amethyst debug wait returns when it gets there.</p><pre><code>amethyst debug runto &lt;at&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>at</code> (string, required): Where to run to: file:line</li></ul><p>Example: <code>amethyst debug runto Pix.cpp:20</code></p>"},"worktrees-and-handoff":{"title":"Worktrees and handoff","html":"<h1>Worktrees and context handoff</h1><p>Amethyst gives you two tools for working across multiple agents cleanly: <strong>git worktrees</strong> so parallel chats do not overwrite each other's files, and <strong>context handoff</strong> so a single chat can switch from one agent to another mid-task.</p><h2>Git worktrees per chat</h2><p>A chat or subagent can run in its own git worktree on its own branch, isolated from your main project folder.</p><h3>Starting a chat in a worktree</h3><ul><li><strong>In the UI:</strong> toggle the worktree button in a new chat's message box before sending the first message.</li><li><strong>From a chat or terminal:</strong> pass <code>--worktree</code> to <code>amethyst chat new</code>, <code>amethyst chat send</code> (on a fresh chat), or <code>amethyst agent run</code>:</li></ul><pre><code>amethyst chat new --worktree \"Refactor the parser\"\namethyst agent run codex \"Write unit tests for the parser\" --worktree</code></pre><p>Requirements: the project folder must be a git repository, have at least one commit, and currently be on a branch (not detached <code>HEAD</code>).</p><h3>Where worktrees live and how branches are named</h3><ul><li><strong>Folder:</strong> worktrees are created outside your project folder, by default under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Amethyst\\worktrees</code>. You can move this folder (for example, to the same drive as your repos) in <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents -&gt; worktrees folder</strong>.</li><li><strong>Branch name:</strong> by default a chat worktree creates a branch named <code>chat/{chat}</code> (using the chat's code). You can customize this pattern with <code>amethyst git branch-names --chat \"ai/{chat}\"</code> or in Settings.</li></ul><h3>Copying gitignored files with <code>.worktreeinclude</code></h3><p>Git worktrees only check out tracked files, so gitignored files like <code>.env</code>, local config files, or secrets are not there by default.</p><p>Add a <code>.worktreeinclude</code> file at the root of your repository listing gitignored paths (using <code>.gitignore</code> syntax) that Amethyst should copy into every new worktree:</p><pre><code>.env\n.env.local\nconfig/local.json</code></pre><p>Amethyst copies matching gitignored files (up to 2,000 files) whenever it creates a worktree for a chat, a branch, or a pull request.</p><h3>Cleaning up a worktree</h3><p>Amethyst never merges a worktree branch automatically. When the work is ready, merge or cherry-pick the branch yourself (or ask the agent to), then remove the worktree from the Source Control panel. Amethyst refuses to remove a worktree that still has uncommitted changes so nothing is lost by accident.</p><h2>Context handoff (switching agents mid-chat)</h2><p>You can switch any chat from one agent to another midway — for example, plan with Claude, switch to Codex for an implementation pass, or hand off to Antigravity for a second opinion.</p><p>When you switch agents, Amethyst builds a structured handoff from the chat log and passes it to the new agent automatically. The handoff includes:</p><ul><li>Which agent it is taking over from, and the working folder (including the worktree branch if the chat is in one).</li><li>The project's rules file if one applies.</li><li>The list of files the chat has edited so far.</li><li>The latest plan checklist, if the chat had one.</li><li>The conversation and one-line summaries of the tools run since the new agent last spoke in this chat.</li><li>A path to the full, uncut chat log on disk if the conversation was long enough to be trimmed to fit the handoff budget.</li></ul><p>The new agent is told to check files and <code>git status</code> on disk before relying on older state, and not to repeat the conversation back to you.</p>"},"git-and-github":{"title":"Git, PRs, and issues","html":"<h1>Git, pull requests, and issues</h1><p>Amethyst's <code>git</code>, <code>pr</code>, and <code>issue</code> commands let you and your agents inspect repositories, manage GitHub pull requests and issues, customize branch naming, and open visual views directly in Amethyst's UI.</p><h2>Opening views in Amethyst from an agent</h2><p>Coding agents can already run <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code>, but they cannot open tabs in your editor on their own. These commands open Amethyst's UI for you when you ask your agent to show you something:</p><ul><li><code>amethyst git diff &lt;path&gt; [--a &lt;ref&gt;] [--b &lt;ref&gt;]</code> — open a file's diff in Amethyst's diff tab (last commit against disk by default, or between two refs).</li><li><code>amethyst git history [path] [--ref &lt;ref&gt;]</code> — open commit history in Source Control for the branch, another ref, or a single file.</li><li><code>amethyst git compare &lt;ref&gt; [--base &lt;ref&gt;]</code> — open a branch comparison showing its commits and changed files against the base branch.</li><li><code>amethyst pr open &lt;n&gt;</code> — open pull request <code>#n</code> in its own tab in Amethyst.</li><li><code>amethyst issue open &lt;n&gt;</code> — open issue <code>#n</code> in its own tab in Amethyst.</li><li><code>amethyst chat review &lt;code&gt; [--file &lt;path&gt;]</code> — open a chat's edit Review window with Keep / Reject per file.</li></ul><p>Example prompt for an agent:</p><p>&gt; Compare my <code>chat/q20</code> branch against <code>main</code> in Amethyst with <code>amethyst git compare chat/q20</code>, and open PR 42 with <code>amethyst pr open 42</code>.</p><p>These UI-opening commands (plus <code>git status</code>, <code>git branch-names</code>, <code>pr list</code>, <code>pr view</code>, <code>issue list</code>, and <code>issue view</code>) are also exposed through the MCP server.</p><h2>Customizing branch naming patterns</h2><p>You can view or change how Amethyst names new branches for a project:</p><pre><code>amethyst git branch-names\namethyst git branch-names --chat \"ai/{chat}\" --issue \"feature/{n}-{title}\" --pr \"review/{n}\" --new \"{user}/\"\namethyst git branch-names --reset</code></pre><p>Available placeholders:</p><ul><li><code>{chat}</code> — the chat's code (valid in <code>--chat</code>).</li><li><code>{n}</code> — the issue or pull request number (required in <code>--issue</code> and <code>--pr</code>).</li><li><code>{title}</code> — the slugified issue title (valid in <code>--issue</code>).</li><li><code>{user}</code> — your git <code>user.name</code> (valid in all four).</li></ul><p>Defaults: <code>chat/{chat}</code>, <code>{n}-{title}</code>, <code>pr/{n}</code>, and empty for <code>--new</code>.</p><h2>Git commands</h2><p>Run <code>amethyst help git</code> for every flag. Most commands accept <code>--repo &lt;folder&gt;</code> when a project contains submodules or nested repositories (<code>amethyst git repos</code> lists them).</p><ul><li><code>amethyst git status</code> — branch, remote tracking, ahead/behind counts, active merge/rebase, and changed files.</li><li><code>amethyst git commit [message] [--push] [--amend] [--force] [--skip &lt;a,b&gt;]</code> — commit staged files, or all changes (leaving secrets out) if nothing is staged.</li><li><code>amethyst git fetch</code> / <code>pull [--how rebase|merge]</code> / <code>push [--lease]</code> / <code>sync [--how rebase|merge]</code> — sync with the remote.</li><li><code>amethyst git branch &lt;name&gt; [--create] [--from &lt;ref&gt;] [--changes stash]</code> — switch to or create a branch.</li><li><code>amethyst git abort</code> / <code>continue</code> / <code>skip</code> — manage an in-progress merge, rebase, cherry-pick, or revert.</li><li><code>amethyst git clone &lt;url-or-owner/repo&gt; [into] [--name &lt;project&gt;] [--no-add]</code> — clone with submodules and add as a project in the current workspace. While <strong>chats answer other chats' permission asks</strong> is off, a clone, pull, sync, branch switch, <code>pr checkout</code> or <code>issue start</code> by a chat, an agent outside Amethyst or a terminal is refused when it would bring in an agent's settings or hooks (such as <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code> or <code>.github/hooks/</code>); from Amethyst's window it works as always.</li><li><code>amethyst git tag &lt;name&gt; [--at &lt;ref&gt;] [--message &lt;text&gt;] [--push]</code> — create and optionally push a tag.</li><li><code>amethyst git release &lt;tag&gt; [--title &lt;text&gt;] [--notes &lt;text&gt;]</code> — publish a GitHub release for a tag.</li><li><code>amethyst git log [path] [--q &lt;query&gt;] [--ref &lt;ref&gt;] [--skip &lt;n&gt;]</code> — search commits (supports <code>author:name</code> and <code>code:text</code>).</li><li><code>amethyst git blame &lt;path&gt;</code> — line-by-line commit and author info.</li></ul><h2>Pull requests and issues</h2><p>Run <code>amethyst help pr</code> and <code>amethyst help issue</code> for every flag.</p><h3>Pull requests</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst pr list [--show open|mine|review]</code> — list pull requests with branch, draft, review, and check status.</li><li><code>amethyst pr view &lt;n&gt;</code> — inspect one pull request, its checks, merge blockers, and description.</li><li><code>amethyst pr open &lt;n&gt;</code> — open it in Amethyst.</li><li><code>amethyst pr create &lt;title&gt; [--body &lt;md&gt;] [--base &lt;branch&gt;] [--draft]</code> — push the current branch if needed and open a PR (an issue number in the branch name is linked with <code>Fixes #N</code>).</li><li><code>amethyst pr comment &lt;n&gt; &lt;body&gt;</code> — comment on a PR.</li><li><code>amethyst pr review &lt;n&gt; --how approve|changes|comment [--body &lt;text&gt;]</code> — submit a review.</li><li><code>amethyst pr merge &lt;n&gt; --how squash|merge|rebase [--delete-branch] [--auto]</code> — merge a PR.</li><li><code>amethyst pr checkout &lt;n&gt; [--worktree]</code> — check out a PR locally, or in its own worktree folder.</li></ul><h3>Issues</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst issue list [--show open|mine|closed] [--q &lt;words&gt;]</code> — list or search issues.</li><li><code>amethyst issue view &lt;n&gt;</code> — inspect an issue, its labels, linked PRs, and comments.</li><li><code>amethyst issue open &lt;n&gt;</code> — open it in Amethyst.</li><li><code>amethyst issue new &lt;title&gt; [--body &lt;md&gt;] [--labels &lt;a,b&gt;] [--me]</code> — create an issue.</li><li><code>amethyst issue comment &lt;n&gt; &lt;body&gt;</code> — comment on an issue.</li><li><code>amethyst issue close &lt;n&gt; [--reason completed|\"not planned\"]</code> — close an issue.</li><li><code>amethyst issue start &lt;n&gt; [--where here|folder]</code> — create a branch named for the issue, link it on GitHub, and switch to it (or open it in a worktree folder with <code>--where folder</code>).</li></ul>"},"unreal-debugger":{"title":"Unreal Engine and debugger","html":"<h1>Unreal Engine and the debugger</h1><p>Amethyst includes beta Unreal Engine C++ support on Windows: building with the engine named in your <code>.uproject</code>, live C++ diagnostics, and a native Windows debugger (Microsoft DbgEng) that both you and your agents can drive.</p><h2>Setup</h2><p>1. Turn on Unreal Engine support in <strong>Settings -&gt; Unreal</strong>.</p><p>2. Download the debugger in <strong>Settings -&gt; Unreal -&gt; debugger</strong> (about 17 MB, requires accepting Microsoft's license), or press <code>F5</code> the first time you debug. Only you can download or remove the debugger; agents cannot install it for you.</p><h2>Letting an agent debug with you</h2><p>As with all Amethyst commands, chats are not told about <code>amethyst debug</code> automatically. Ask your agent in plain words when you want it to inspect or drive a debug session, for example:</p><p>&gt; Set a breakpoint at <code>Source/MyGame/Player.cpp:142</code> when <code>Health &lt; 0</code> using <code>amethyst debug break</code>, wait for it to hit with <code>amethyst debug wait</code>, then inspect the stack and local variables.</p><p>&gt; Attach to my running Unreal Editor with <code>amethyst debug start --attach</code> and check why <code>InitInventory</code> crashes.</p><p>Every breakpoint and action an agent takes is marked as an agent action in the debug column so you can see what it did.</p><h2>Commands</h2><p>Run <code>amethyst help debug</code> for the full reference.</p><h3>Starting, waiting, and stopping</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst debug start</code> — build the Editor target and launch Unreal Editor (<code>DebugGame Editor</code>) under the debugger, or attach if Unreal Editor is already open on the project.</li></ul><p>  - <code>--game</code> — debug the game in its own window (Unreal Editor with <code>-game</code>).</p><p>  - <code>--attach</code> — attach to an already-open Unreal Editor without launching a new one.</p><p>  - <code>--no-build</code> — skip building before starting.</p><ul><li><code>amethyst debug status</code> — report whether the debugger is idle, building, running, or stopped (and where/why), plus the breakpoint count.</li><li><code>amethyst debug wait [--ms &lt;ms&gt;]</code> — wait until the program stops (breakpoint, step, crash, failed <code>check()</code>/<code>ensure()</code>) or debugging ends. Defaults to <code>30000</code> ms (up to <code>900000</code> ms); call again if it is still running.</li><li><code>amethyst debug stop [--end]</code> — detach by default so Unreal Editor keeps running with your unsaved work. Passing <code>--end</code> terminates the process and discards unsaved work in Unreal Editor.</li></ul><h3>Breakpoints and execution control</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst debug break [file:line]</code> — list all breakpoints when called with no arguments, or set one at <code>file:line</code> (a project-relative path, or just the filename if it is unique).</li></ul><p>  - <code>--if \"&lt;expr&gt;\"</code> — conditional C++ breakpoint, e.g. <code>amethyst debug break Player.cpp:42 --if \"Health &lt; 0\"</code>.</p><p>  - <code>--remove &lt;file:line&gt;</code> — remove a breakpoint.</p><ul><li><code>amethyst debug continue</code> — resume execution.</li><li><code>amethyst debug pause</code> — pause the running program (preferring Unreal's GameThread).</li><li><code>amethyst debug step [over|into|out]</code> — step one line (<code>over</code> by default).</li><li><code>amethyst debug runto &lt;file:line&gt;</code> — run until <code>file:line</code> using a one-time breakpoint that is not saved.</li><li><code>amethyst debug goto &lt;file:line&gt;</code> — set the next statement to another line in the same function (skipped or repeated code is not undone).</li></ul><h3>Inspecting state while stopped</h3><ul><li><code>amethyst debug stack [--all] [--thread &lt;id&gt;]</code> — show the call stack with frame numbers (<code>#0</code>, <code>#1</code>, ...). Long engine frame runs are folded unless you pass <code>--all</code>, and Blueprint frames appear as <code>[Blueprint] BP_Name</code>.</li><li><code>amethyst debug vars [--frame &lt;n&gt;] [--depth &lt;1-4&gt;]</code> — show local variables and <code>this</code> as an indented tree, with Unreal type formatting (<code>FString</code> text, <code>TArray</code> count and items, <code>UObject</code> name and class). Defaults to frame <code>0</code> and depth <code>2</code>.</li><li><code>amethyst debug eval &lt;expr&gt; [--frame &lt;n&gt;] [--depth &lt;0-3&gt;]</code> — evaluate a C++ expression (for example, <code>this-&gt;Weapon</code> or <code>Items.Num</code>).</li></ul><h2>Read-only mode for agents</h2><p>In <strong>Settings -&gt; Agents -&gt; Amethyst tools</strong>, you can toggle <strong>agents can control the debugger</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>On (default):</strong> agents can start and stop debugging, set/remove breakpoints, step, continue, pause, set the next statement, and evaluate expressions with calls or assignments.</li><li><strong>Off:</strong> agents can still read <code>debug status</code>, <code>debug stack</code>, <code>debug vars</code>, <code>debug wait</code>, list breakpoints (<code>debug break</code> with no arguments), and evaluate read-only expressions (no calls or assignments), but cannot change execution or state.</li></ul>"},"support":{"title":"Support and security","html":"<h1>Support</h1><p>Ask questions and report bugs on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/yppAbHmMXG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Amethyst Discord</a>: questions in <strong>#help</strong>, bugs in</p><p><strong>#bug-reports</strong>, ideas in <strong>#feedback</strong>. Check <strong>#known-issues</strong> first, the problem may already be listed there.</p><h2>A good bug report</h2><ul><li><strong>Amethyst version</strong>: settings show it.</li><li><strong>Windows version</strong>: Windows 10 or 11.</li><li><strong>Agent involved</strong>, if any: Claude, Codex or Antigravity.</li><li><strong>Steps to reproduce</strong>, numbered.</li><li><strong>What you expected</strong> and <strong>what happened</strong> instead.</li><li><strong>How often</strong>: every time, sometimes or once.</li><li><strong>Logs or screenshots</strong>, if you have them.</li></ul><p>Before posting logs or screenshots, remove API keys, access tokens, passwords, private code and anything else you</p><p>don't want public.</p><p>Problems with an agent itself (Claude Code, the Codex CLI or the Antigravity CLI) go to its provider. Report them here</p><p>too if you're not sure, and I'll tell you.</p><p>Security problems don't go in public channels: see <strong>SECURITY.md</strong>.</p>"}};
